    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d602">

                <pb facs="0720" n="622" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg622"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/1321: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Tenney</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-01-27T17:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 27,
                                1920—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received January 27—1:46
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>26. Referring to the Legation’s telegram[s] January 19, 4 p.m.<anchor n="72"
                        xml:id="d602-f1"/> and January 20, 11 a.m.<anchor n="73" xml:id="d602-f2"/>
                    The Chinese Ministry of Finance is impatient for the American reply to the loan
                    proposal, the Chinese [New Year] settlement debts being only three weeks off.
                    The British, French and Japanese Legations have informed the Chinese that
                    nothing can be done until I receive instructions. The report is circulating
                    among the Chinese that Americans are holding up the loan and American prestige
                    is thereby suffering. I would appreciate definite instructions so that I may
                    announce the American decision either for or against the loan.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tenney</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d602-f1" n="72" xml:id="d602-f1-content">
                <p>Probably refers to Legation’s undated telegram, <ref target="#pg621">p.
                    621</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d602-f2" n="73" xml:id="d602-f2-content">
